      "details": "<p><span>The relevant line of the Cyprus Stele of the Assyrian king Sargon II (r. 721-705 BC), col. iv 52, might be read </span><span>as\u00a0[</span><em>i-na</em><span>? ZAG? KUR?].</span><em>ba-il</em><span>-\u1e2aAR-</span><em>ri</em><span><em>\u00a0</em>KUR-</span><span><em>i</em>\u00a0</span><span>(\"</span><span class=\"r \">[</span><em><span class=\"i \"><span class=\"w\">beside/facing\u00a0</span><span class=\"w\">Mount</span></span></em><span class=\"i \">]</span><span><span class=\"i \">\u00a0<em><span class=\"w\">Ba'il-hurri</span></em></span>,\u00a0</span><span class=\"w\">a</span><span>\u00a0</span><span class=\"w\">mountain\") or as <span>[<em>ina</em>? \u00c9? d?].</span><span><em>ba-il</em> <em>\u1e2bur-ri</em>\u00a0</span><span>KUR-</span><em>i</em><span>\u00a0(\"[in the temple of the god Ba'al of Mount Hurri\")</span>. Thus, the name, assuming it is one, is either Hurri or Ba'il-hurri (exact reading uncertain). Note that it is possible that\u00a0\u1e2aAR-<em>ri</em>\u00a0KUR-<span>i</span>\u00a0could be interpreted as\u00a0<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-f0006b62-7fff-9849-6dc0-06fd1206dc6f\"><em>hurr\u012b \u0161ad\u00ee</em>\u00a0<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-015e9f8e-7fff-b96c-9774-217afa19e7cc\">\u201cmines\u201d or \u201cmountain ravines.\u201d</span></span></span></p>\r\n<p><span class=\"w\"><span><span>The exact reading of the cuneiform sign\u00a0<span>\u1e2aAR is uncertain. It could be read as either <em>hur</em> or <em>har</em>.</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n<p>\u00a0</p>\r\n<p>\u00a0</p>",
